WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.470
Film Courage: People who wait for a filmmaking
career will never have one? Without of course

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:11.829
naming any names, can you think of some people
in your life who are very talented but who

00:00:11.829 --> 00:00:17.869
are waiting? Maybe this frustrates you? Maybe
you see the opportunity in front of them but

00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:20.250
they won’t take it for whatever reason?

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.849
Kenneth Castillo, Filmmaker: Yes, mostly colleagues.
I won’t even say friends, just people that

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:32.480
you run into. But what I’ve come to realize
is that (particularly those filmmakers who’ve

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.260
come out of film school) you are not going
to have a crew of 30 on your next film. They

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:43.350
come out&nbsp;with these great thesis films and
they want to get their feature made. But in

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.760
this age of independent filmmaking, no one
is going to give you a million dollars to

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.320
do your thesis film.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.670
And when I don’t see them working or trying
to start from scratch whether it’s a feature

00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:01.820
film (whatever it is) to continue to do the
craft until you get that. I’m not saying

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.040
it’s not possible but I’m really blown
away by filmmakers I know that have a great

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.650
thesis film from film school and are waiting
to make their feature film. I’m like “Why

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.690
aren’t you doing something now?” “I
don’t want to film something on my phone.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:19.680
I don’t want to shoot something with a three-person
crew.” I’m like “What are you waiting

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.810
for?”

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:28.750
It’s not something I can tell people. You’ve
got to figure that out on your own. But if

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.700
you’ve been 10 years now (and I know filmmakers
that are 10 years out of film school) still

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.642
thinking they are going to get 3 million dollars
to produce their first film based off of their

00:01:37.642 --> 00:01:38.642
thesis.

00:01:38.642 --> 00:01:44.600
Film Courage: Why do you think this is? Do
you think they feel they’re at a different

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.650
status level? They don’t want people...they
want to have investors?

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:54.800
Kenneth: I think it’s a combination of that
and people in this industry. I think everyone

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.810
looks at their trajectory going like this
[consistent upward line] and it doesn’t.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.350
You started with a thesis and you have a crew
of thirty and all of this millions of dollars

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:07.830
worth of equipment that the school provides
for you. You get out, do you really want to

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:17.210
film your short movie on an iPhone? The ones
that work do. Because that’s ego. In my

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:22.639
experience having done as much as I’ve done
what I’ve found is there are certain film

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:27.909
schools (and I’ve hired certain film school
students)...but I won’t say the ones where

00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:31.251
they have the least discipline but the ones
with the MOST discipline have come from the

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:36.510
Los Angeles Film School. They have come with
the discipline to work. It didn’t matter

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:41.389
how good their thesis film was. If my crew
was small or whatever, they wanted to work,

00:02:41.389 --> 00:02:43.829
they wanted to learn and they wanted to produce.

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:48.060
Film Courage: I know there’s a lot of backlash
on Millennials that they don’t want to work

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.079
and things like that. I haven’t always seen
this. I’ve seen work ethics differ across

00:02:52.079 --> 00:02:58.010
many generations (even in Generation X I’ve
seen varied work ethics). Do you think it’s

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.559
generational or a mindset?

00:02:59.559 --> 00:03:05.379
Kenneth: It’s definitely a mindset, I don’t
find it generational at all. If this is what

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:09.419
you want to do, you’re going to do it and
you’re going to do it as often as you possibly

00:03:09.419 --> 00:03:13.019
can. The thing I say to people who want to
be writers, you have to be writing everyday.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:17.260
If you want to be a director or filmmaker,
you should be trying to make something or

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.709
shoot something every month, every week if
you can, but at least every month.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:28.269
It’s a craft, it’s like building a deck.
The first step you build is not going to be

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:33.340
as fancy or as good. But the more you do it
the better you get at it. And filmmaking is

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.189
no different so I don’t really subscribe
to that philosophy of “Well I’m waiting

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:41.430
for this. I’m waiting for the right money,
I’m waiting for the right people.” No.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.549
I’m not a subscriber to that. You just kind
of have to go forward. If that’s all you’re

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:50.760
banking on, if that’s all you have is that
one script that you want to make perfect,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.930
you’re going to have a difficult time.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:04:00.510
Film Courage: Do you think the DIY mindset...because
there are certain people who are really self-generating,

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.989
they just do, they rely on themselves. They
don’t expect any handouts and they just

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:08.839
want to do something themselves. Whereas there
are others where maybe they’ve had more

00:04:08.839 --> 00:04:13.430
coddling or whatever there is, do you think
that there’s a clash? Do you think those

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:14.480
two mindsets clash?

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:24.090
Kenneth: Only on a panel. Yes because to me
it just comes down to the work. Can you produce?

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:33.000
So it can...there are certain filmmakers we
follow. I think filmmakers try to model their

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.670
careers after other filmmakers. That’s not
something I’ve ever done. I’ve never looked

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.910
at someone and said “This is how I’m going
to do it like they did. Or “I’m going

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:44.960
to do this and this is how it’s going to
happen.” My thing has always been just how

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.630
do I get my stuff made right now?

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:53.090
If I wrote a ten million dollar script I would
have it but that wouldn’t be the first thing

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:57.310
I would try to get made. I would have a fifty
thousand dollar script. I would have a ten

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:03.530
thousand dollar script. I would have different
things. What can I do right now? And if that

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.930
does take, what do I have back here that is
ready to go?

